"""Career catalogue used by Career Discovery.

Two kinds of careers:
1. Careers that exist in the job-postings data (Data Analyst, Data Engineer, ...).
   Their SKILLS come from the postings (pipeline/profiles.py); this file only adds the
   interest profile, description and education info, which postings do not contain.
2. Curated careers outside the dataset (teaching, marketing, design, research, ...).
   The 2023 dataset has no postings for them, so their skill lists are CURATED from
   general occupational knowledge (O*NET-style). They are always labelled
   source="curated" so the site never presents them as dataset results.

Interest codes use the Holland / RIASEC model, the standard in career guidance:
R realistic (hands-on, building), I investigative (analysing, researching),
A artistic (creating, designing), S social (helping, teaching), E enterprising
(leading, persuading), C conventional (organising, detail).
The first letter is the strongest.

Fields
  category      one of CATEGORIES
  riasec        e.g. "ICR"
  work_with     subset of people, data, technology, ideas, creative
  environments  subset of office, remote, classroom, studio, lab, field
  structure     0 = clear, structured tasks ... 1 = lots of creative freedom
  skills        (skill, importance 1-3), curated careers only
"""
from __future__ import annotations

CATEGORIES = ["Data & Analytics", "Software & Cloud", "Education", "Marketing", "Creative & Design",
              "Emerging Technology", "Research & Environment", "Business & Operations"]

# Emerging-technology careers are flagged by field, not by made-up numbers.
EMERGING_CATEGORIES = {"Emerging Technology"}

